            {
              "id": "glog-82a527c7-a9f3-42a4-984e-c2ec378b9b19",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the size of the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, the size of the `ImgSize` object may be larger than the buffer `buf`, leading to a buffer overflow vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by dynamically allocating memory for the buffer based on the size of the data, or by using functions that automatically handle buffer sizes, such as `strncpy` or `snprintf`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t dataSize = sizeof(ImgSize);\nif (dataSize > sizeof(buf)) {\n    // Handle error: data too large for buffer\n} else {\n    memcpy(buf, &(meta_data.second->get_img_sizes_batch()[i]), dataSize);\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, we first check if the size of the `ImgSize` object is larger than the buffer. If it is, we handle the error appropriately. If it's not, we proceed with the `memcpy` operation.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet appears to depend on the following libraries:\n\n- `<cstring>`: for the `memcpy` function\n- `<vector>` or `<array>`: if `get_img_sizes_batch()` returns a vector or array\n- Any libraries required by the `ImgSize` type and the `meta_data` object\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not be accessible at all times."
