            {
              "id": "glog-c0027d95-87f9-4522-9cb8-2ce94ae67f5f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nIn C++ programming, using weak or non-cryptographic random number generators can lead to vulnerabilities, especially in security-sensitive applications. The `random_shuffle` function, which is part of the C++ Standard Library, uses a non-cryptographic random number generator by default. This can result in predictable shuffling, which may be exploited in scenarios where randomness is critical, such as in cryptographic operations or game logic.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a cryptographically secure random number generator. In C++, the `<random>` library provides facilities to generate random numbers with better randomness quality. Specifically, the `std::shuffle` function can be used with a random number engine that provides cryptographic security, such as `std::random_device` or `std::mt19937` seeded with a high-entropy source.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `random_shuffle` with `std::shuffle` and use a secure random number engine. Here's an example of how to do this:\n\n```cpp\n#include <algorithm>\n#include <random>\n#include <vector>\n#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector<int> data = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};\n\n    // Create a random number engine with a high-entropy source\n    std::random_device rd;\n    std::mt19937 gen(rd());\n\n    // Use std::shuffle with the secure random number engine\n    std::shuffle(data.begin(), data.end(), gen);\n\n    // Output the shuffled data\n    for (int n : data) {\n        std::cout << n << ' ';\n    }\n    std::cout << std::endl;\n\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the above code example, the following standard C++ libraries are required:\n\n- `<algorithm>`: Provides the `std::shuffle` function.\n- `<random>`: Provides random number generation facilities.\n- `<vector>`: Used for the dynamic array.\n- `<iostream>`: Used for input and output operations.\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak Pseudo-Random Number Generator (PRNG)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
